  "text": "In a study  during routine preoperative anesthesia preparation [42]. Another study also of elderly hip fracture patients, Balta et al. reported that CRP, one of several CRP-based biomarkers (CRP, CAR, and the CRP-to-lymphocyte ratio), was a good predictor of 30-day mortality after hip fracture surgery, and therefore recommended monitoring of CRP and albuminindicated that high CAR values were associated with an increased mortality risk in hip fracture patients [43]. In the current study, CRP and CAR were also statistically significant predictors of the mortality endpoints, but the HALP score had greater predictive power for both 30-day and 1-year mortality, and its 1-year survival analysis was more statistically significant than those of the other biomarkers. The HALP score can therefore be considered a useful biomarker for distinguishing patients who die within 1 year from those who do not, and could help clinicians make more informed decisions about treatment plans and resource allocation for elderly patients with PFFs. Its predictive value across both short- and long-term mortality underscores its versatility as a prognostic tool, and further validation across diverse patient populations and healthcare settings may establish it as a standard assessment in geriatric orthopedic care."
